  "language": "en",
  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "A heat pump warms your home by moving heat from outdoor air even when that air feels cold. It transports existing heat rather than creating heat from nothing. Outside, cold refrigerant absorbs heat from the air and evaporates into a gas. The heat pump's compressor squeezes this gas, raising its pressure and temperature. The hot gas enters the indoor coil and releases its heat into your home. A fan blows across that coil, carrying the released heat into your rooms. The refrigerant condenses into a liquid as it gives up heat. An expansion valve then lowers its pressure, making it cold again for the outdoor coil. This repeating refrigeration cycle is how the heat pump moves heat indoors.",
  "mean_word_probability": 0.9836725239026345,
